-- Both statements resolve ^cursor against one frozen overlay snapshot: one submission is one barrier, so the second statement cannot observe a different target than the first (todo/02's statement-snapshot law, seen from the batch host).
-- The verbs sequence left to right behind one selection; !^cursor negates the resolved mask, so the blessing lands everywhere the knockback did not.
--! registry ../registries/019-sequenced-effects.reg
--! expect pos = 0 10 25 30 40 50
--! expect tint = None None Red None None None
--! expect shielded = 1 1 0 0 1 0
--! expect gold = 5 10 115 20 25 30
--! expect blessed = 1 1 0 1 1 1

^cursor , Knockback 5 ; Flash :Red ; -Shielded
^cursor , Gold += 100
!^cursor , Blessed = 1
